  "pd": {
    "basis": "published-pre-1930",
    "detail": "Printed in the Fisk Jubilee Singers collections of the early 1870s, the commonly cited first printing being 1873; anonymous African-American spiritual in oral circulation well before that, long out of copyright in the United States.",
    "sourceEdition": "Seward, Theodore F. (ed.), 'Jubilee Songs: As Sung by the Jubilee Singers of Fisk University', Biglow & Main, New York, 1872-73; also J. B. T. Marsh, 'The Story of the Jubilee Singers: With Their Songs'",
    "notes": "Melody and changes only; no lyrics. The traditional refrain is encoded, not any later concert arrangement or recorded treatment. The Jubilee printings are in 2/4; encoded here as 4/4 at half the written tempo, which pairs the written bars two by two and leaves the phrasing untouched."
  },
  "key": "G",
  "mode": "major",
  "meter": "4/4",
  "tempoBPM": 108,
  "tempoRange": [84, 144],
  "feel": "swing",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "notes": "Both sung sections begin on the downbeat, so nothing carries a pickup across a section boundary. The chorus is a driving eighth-note patter over two bars answered by a held note, three times, then the question phrase; the verse is the same patter with a new line of text every two bars. Major-pentatonic (1 2 3 5 6) throughout. The IV bar is written C6 rather than a plain C triad because the phrase ending sustains the 2nd degree (A in G) straight through it; a bare C would leave the only held note in that bar outside the chord. Medium confidence: the rhythm and contour are the traditional ones, the interior passing notes are the common variant rather than one authoritative reading.",
